Spec: Project/Model

Purpose:
  Define how the real software project is represented inside the SDD
  project model — the Project > Domain > Module > Feature > Component
  hierarchy AI resolves before touching source code. This file owns the
  SHAPE of that hierarchy only; the concrete instances (which domains,
  which modules) live in their own sibling files, not here (see
  DoesNotOwn, NavigationFiles).


Owns:
  Project structure (Project > Domain > Module > Feature > Component)
  Feature model (schema)
  Component model (schema)
  Ownership rule
  Project-model navigation order


DoesNotOwn:
  Domain list and domain-level rules        (see ./domains.sdd)
  Module list and module-level contracts    (see ./modules.sdd)
  Routing/index map                         (see ./map.sdd)
  Concrete dependency graph                 (see ./dependencies.sdd)
  Category-level index declarations         (see ./indexes.sdd)
  This project's concrete architecture      (see ./architecture.sdd)
  This project's execution flow             (see ./flows.sdd)
  Global SDD engine rules (chains/tasks/decisions/state/skills/prompts)

  (This file sits one level above domains.sdd/modules.sdd: those two
  already model the Domain and Module layers concretely — bax their own
  Spec headers. This file adds the two layers that were still
  unmodeled, Feature and Component, and states how all five layers
  relate, without re-declaring what domains.sdd/modules.sdd already own.)


Principle:

  The project model MUST represent the software system without
  requiring AI to inspect the entire source tree before understanding
  the system.


ProjectStructure:

  Project > Domain > Module > Feature > Component


Domain:

  A domain represents a coherent business capability.
  Schema and concrete domains: see ./domains.sdd.


Module:

  A module represents a technical or functional boundary inside a
  domain — what it owns, its public surface, its declared dependencies.
  Schema and concrete modules: see ./modules.sdd.


Feature:

  A feature represents executable product behavior inside a module —
  the smallest unit a prompt or task typically targets.

  Example (illustrative only, no concrete feature exists yet):

    payment (module) > refund (feature) > automatic-refund (variant)

  A feature SHOULD declare: purpose, owning module, dependencies, and
  the chain type it runs under (bax chains/chains.sdd -> Stages).


Component:

  A component represents an implementation boundary inside a feature —
  the discipline-level slice of code that actually realizes it.

  MAY include (reusing this project's own established layer vocabulary,
  bax ./architecture.sdd -> Layers, chains/chains.sdd -> Stages):

    DB    database / persistence
    BE    backend / domain logic
    API   API surface
    FE    frontend
    MD    mobile
    QA    tests
    DO    deployment / devops

  A component SHOULD declare: path, owner, responsibility, exposes,
  depends_on (bax CodeMapping below).


Ownership:

  Every project element (domain, module, feature, component) MUST have
  one clear owner.

  Example:

    payments (module)
      owns: payment state, payment rules, payment database model

    refund (feature)
      owns: refund behavior, refund workflow


Navigation:

  AI SHOULD resolve, top to bottom, before reading source code:

    Project > Domain > Module > Feature > Component > Code

  Entry point for this resolution: ./map.sdd (routing only, bax its own
  Purpose) and ./indexes.sdd (category-level fast lookup).


Rules:

  [PM1]
  Every project domain MUST have a project model file (bax
  ./domains.sdd -> Rules for the domain-level requirement this refines).

  [PM2]
  Every domain SHOULD expose its boundaries and declare owned
  components.

  [PM3]
  Project elements (domain/module/feature/component declarations) MUST
  NOT duplicate information owned by global SDD systems (chains, tasks,
  decisions, state, skills) — reference them instead.

  [PM4]
  Feature declarations MUST reference the chain type they run under
  (bax chains/chains.sdd -> Stages) instead of redefining chain
  lifecycle rules.

  [PM5]
  Feature/component declarations MUST reference task and decision rules
  (bax tasks/tasks.sdd, decisions/decisions.sdd) instead of redefining
  their lifecycle.

  [PM6]
  Component code paths MUST be mapped to project ownership (bax
  CodeMapping below).

  [PM7]
  AI SHOULD use this project model to locate code before searching the
  source tree (bax Navigation above).

  [PM8]
  This file MUST NOT redeclare the concrete domain/module list already
  owned by domains.sdd/modules.sdd (bax DoesNotOwn — avoids
  NoDuplication violations, bax architecture/architecture.sdd ->
  Principles).


CodeMapping:

  Every component SHOULD declare:

    path
    owner
    responsibility
    exposes
    depends_on

  (Real paths are lookup references for a future real project tree —
  bax PROJECT.sdd -> Note, prompt/new/14.md: creating backend/frontend/
  mobile/qa/devops directories now is explicitly out of scope.)


Portability:

  This file MUST remain reusable across projects unchanged — it defines
  the SHAPE of the hierarchy, not this project's concrete domains.
  Concrete instances belong to domains.sdd/modules.sdd and future
  project/<domain>/ content, not here.


ResolutionOrder:

  PROJECT > DOMAIN > MODULE > FEATURE > COMPONENT > CODE


NavigationFiles:

  map.sdd:           Project navigation map (routing only)
  architecture.sdd:   This project's concrete architecture
  flows.sdd:          This project's execution flow (bax [PM4])
  dependencies.sdd:    Concrete dependency graph (schema currently)
  indexes.sdd:         Category-level fast navigation index
  domains.sdd:         Domain schema and concrete domain list
  modules.sdd:         Module schema and concrete module list


State: STEP 4 (domains/modules) + STEP 14 (this file, Feature/Component)

Note:
  (prompt/new/36.md — source labeled this "STEP 13", a THIRD consecutive
  reuse of a step number already used by prompt/new/34.md and its own
  prior chunk 35.md; this repo's own sequential count treats it as
  STEP 14, bax ../PROJECT.sdd -> Note.)

  Naming/placement decision: the source proposes this content as
  `project.sdd`, plus full rewrites of `map.sdd`, `dependencies.sdd` and
  `indexes.sdd`. All three of those already exist in this repo with
  different, STEP-4-era content (bax their own Spec headers and Notes,
  prompt/new/21.md) that already fully satisfies their stated scope —
  overwriting them would lose STEP 4's reset history and violate this
  repo's own NoDuplication/SingleSourceOfTruth principles for no gain.
  They were left untouched; this file's `NavigationFiles` section
  references them instead of restating their content (bax [PM8]).

  Rule IDs: the source used a bare `[P1]-[P10]` range, which collides
  with `prompts/prompts.sdd`'s own `[P#]` range (bax PROJECT.sdd ->
  Directories -> prompts). Renumbered to `[PM1]-[PM8]`
  ("ProjectModel") to read unambiguously next to this repo's other
  prefixes ([R] PROJECT rules, [C] chains, [T] tasks, [D] decisions,
  [S] state, [AP] architecture principles, [P] prompts). Two of the
  source's ten rules ([P4]/skills-reference and [P9]/CodeMapping-only)
  were folded into neighboring rules rather than kept as separates,
  since they restated the same NoDuplication point already covered.

  Deliberate departures from the source spec, per the standing "apply,
  don't copy" rule: Domain/Module sections were shortened to pointers
  into the already-existing `domains.sdd`/`modules.sdd` rather than
  redefining them here (avoids a second source of truth); Component's
  "MAY include: backend/api/frontend/mobile/database/qa/devops" prose
  was replaced with this project's own already-established DB/BE/API/
  FE/MD/QA/DO vocabulary (bax project/architecture.sdd -> Layers,
  chains/chains.sdd -> Stages) instead of introducing a second,
  differently-worded component list; added an explicit DoesNotOwn
  section (absent from the source) to make the boundary against the
  five sibling files explicit, matching this repo's established
  per-file convention (bax architecture/principles.sdd -> DoesNotOwn).
